        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_69c014bc-c939-4181-9282-4f5f66d0e9e5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00034">For questions 9 - 10, use the graph below.</para>
            <para id="para-00035">
                      <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_10_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00036">Written in the form \(f(x)=a \cdot b^x\), what is the value of \(a\)<emphasis> </emphasis>for each function?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            
            <para id="para-00037">Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.3. 
    </para>
            <para id="para-00038">The correct answer is 160.
    </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3781b9cd-ca4c-4fe0-81eb-5dffa0788c7c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00039">Which graph decreases the most quickly?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            
            <para id="para-00040">Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00041">The correct answer is Graph C.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_899336df-b768-443d-b6de-9feb1fd0e752" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00042">The graphs of \(y=50 \cdot 2^x\), \(y=200 \cdot 2^x\), and \(y=100 \cdot 2^x\) are shown.</para>
            <para id="para-00043">Which is the graph of \(y=100 \cdot 2^x\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            
            <para id="para-00044">Try again. Revisit activities 5.12.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00045">The correct answer is Graph B.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d6125db6-2ebc-469e-a89d-2be4e6a30b80" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00046">For questions 12 – 14, use the graph below:</para>
            <para id="para-00047">
                      <media alt="Graph of six exponential functions.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_10_04.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00048">Which of the functions graphed, \(y=a \cdot b^x\), has the smallest value for \(a\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            
            <para id="para-00049">Try again. Revisit activities 5.12.3 – 5.12.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00050">The correct answer is F. Since F has the lowest \(y\)-intercept, it has the smallest value of \(a\).

    </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_47391196-f897-4c2a-af5f-a545678afce2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <para id="para-00051">Which of the functions graphed, \(y=a \cdot b^x\), has the lowest value for \(b\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00013">
            
            <para id="para-00052">Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.2. 
    </para>
            <para id="para-00053">The correct answer is C. Since C is decaying the fastest, it has the lowest value for \(b\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="af7ac488-3fd7-429e-aafc-f9a4fdf1d9ca" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00014">
            <para id="para-00054">What is the equation of the horizontal asymptote for \(f(x)=5 \cdot 2^x-3\)?</para>
            <para id="para-00055">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00056">\(y=2\)</para>
            <para id="para-00057">\(y=3\)</para>
            <para id="para-00058">\(y=-3\)</para>
            <para id="para-00059">\(y=5\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00014">
            
            <para id="para-00060">Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.2. 
    </para>
            <para id="para-00061">The correct answer is \(y= -3\). The –3 in the function shifts the exponential down 3.
    </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
